    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nExtended the business-day rule in `NormalizeOwnerRegistrationDate.java` to also roll forward past public holidays:\n\n- Added the fixed holiday list as a `Set<LocalDate>`: `2026-01-01`, `2026-01-26`, `2026-04-25`, `2026-12-25`, `2026-12-28`.\n- Replaced the fixed Saturday-+2 / Sunday-+1 branches with a loop that advances one day at a time while the date is a weekend **or** a holiday. This naturally handles runs of consecutive non-business days \u2014 e.g. a date landing on Fri 2026-12-25 (holiday) rolls to Sat, Sun, then Mon 2026-12-28 (also a holiday), then to Tue 2026-12-29 (the next non-holiday business day).\n\nThe weekend behavior is unchanged for the existing cases, and `isBusinessDay` keeps the logic in one place.\n\n## Verification\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The acceptance test (`coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay`) passes, asserting the default registration date is never a weekend.\n\nOne note: the only test in the suite is the black-box acceptance invariant (never Sat/Sun) \u2014 there's no test that directly exercises a holiday-landing date, since the comment notes the holiday calendar and server clock can't be driven black-box. The implementation follows the spec's rule exactly regardless.",
